Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Guard the psetdef(rd) cast after a non-halting type mismatch (fpc 0.99)
License: expat

In compiler/tcadd.pas, inside the setdef-operand arm of the add/op
handler, control can fall through after CGMessage reports a
type-mismatch between `rd' and the setdef `ld'.  The fallthrough then
does:

    if (psetdef(ld)^.settype<>smallset) and
       (psetdef(rd)^.settype=smallset) then

`rd' at this point is whatever the mismatched operand was -- it need
not be a setdef at all.  When it isn't (in practice it's commonly an
enumdef / orddef), `psetdef(rd)^.settype' reads a field that the
actual record doesn't have, at a meaningless offset.  tsetdef and
the other defs are siblings, not a hierarchy: the cast is just
wrong.  Native FPC tolerated it because the bytes at that offset
were a valid `tsettype' value either way; under tp2cc the cast is
rejected at runtime as a downcast onto a foreign record type.

Fix: guard the `psetdef(rd)' cast with `rd^.deftype = setdef'.

--- a/compiler/tcadd.pas
+++ b/compiler/tcadd.pas
@@ -648,7 +648,14 @@

                 { if the destination is not a smallset then insert a typeconv
                   which loads a smallset into a normal set }
-                if (psetdef(ld)^.settype<>smallset) and
+                { rd may not actually be a setdef here: when a type-mismatch
+                  was reported above via CGMessage, control falls through
+                  without halting, leaving rd as an unrelated def (enum,
+                  ord, ...).  The `psetdef(rd)' cast below was wrong in
+                  that case -- the unrelated def has no `settype' field.
+                  Guard the cast explicitly. }
+                if (rd^.deftype=setdef) and
+                   (psetdef(ld)^.settype<>smallset) and
                    (psetdef(rd)^.settype=smallset) then
                  begin
                    if (p^.right^.treetype=setconstn) then
